{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1810 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ce97b1ef21d6cbac6bd5b66776d901cd6342b81a201c7a2278cd5278c2a54f96" :edb/db-before-t 1809 :edb/db-after-t 1810 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535122 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:21:31.609Z" 13194139535122 true] [17592186228326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332249 13194139535122 true] [17592186228326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332250 13194139535122 true] [17592186228326 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332250 13194139535122 true] [17592186228879 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/7e54c9613c18a0ea26df6a4088598b566e532fba555dc08db0bcfd539b4c64d5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 true] [17592186228879 1100 "Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/ec87d268cc3be6e49308a705949867b5.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 false] [17592186228879 1145 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $80\\,\\Omega$, and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(3t) = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin(3t)\n$$" 13194139535122 false] [17592186228879 1145 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(3t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $80\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.0025} = 400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(3t) = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin(3t).\n$$" 13194139535122 true] [17592186228880 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/424cb52325c95f03708ed9ae5fe1d41e7b0d1c8bb8878e1dd8dc002aef214481.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 true] [17592186228880 1100 "Find an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/b144c85dbd922b70e678db80c285015e.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 false] [17592186228880 1145 "The current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega$.So,\n$V_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.00125} = 800q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24 = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 800q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac3{10}\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-10t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha$.\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n$0 + 10\\alpha = \\dfrac3{10} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\alpha = \\dfrac3{100}$\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac3{100}$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-10t} + \\dfrac3{100}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}\n$$\n\non the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition\n\n$$\nq(0) = \\dfrac{1}{50}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(0) &= \\frac{1}{50} \\\\\nAe^{-10(0)} + \\frac{3}{100} &= \\frac{1}{50} \\\\\nA &= -\\frac{1}{100}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nq(t) &= -\\frac{1}{100}e^{-10t} + \\frac{3}{100} \\\\\n&= 1 100 (3 - e - 10 t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = -\\dfrac1{100}e^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac3{100}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty$, the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139535122 false] [17592186228880 1145 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 80\\,\\Omega.$So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 80I = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{0.00125} = 800q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24 = 80\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 800q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q = \\dfrac3{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 10q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $q_p = \\alpha$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_p}{\\mathrm{d}t} = (\\alpha)' = 0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0 + 10\\alpha = \\dfrac3{10}\n  \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n  \\alpha = \\dfrac3{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $q_p(t) = \\dfrac3{100}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-10t} + \\dfrac3{100}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\dfrac{1}{50}\\,\\textrm{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0) = \\dfrac{1}{50},$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{3}{100} & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{3}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}(3-{e}^{-10t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c = -\\dfrac1{100}e^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The constant $q_p = \\dfrac3{100}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to\\infty,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139535122 true] [17592186228881 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/962694e1706c47a4b060da6636e0b6e2f1653cb94137dca8dc59781cf74bd252.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C,$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 true] [17592186228881 1100 "Find the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6701/Source/Images/f517f979d06508ba19c194b407cfe616.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 false] [17592186228881 1145 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, and a capacitor with capacitance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}\n$$\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t)\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t)$.\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute\n$q_{p}|= α\\cos (6t) + β\\sin (6t),; \\frac{dq_{p}}{dt}|=-6α\\sin (6t) + 6β\\cos (6t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-6α\\sin (6t) + 6β\\cos (6t)) + 5(α\\cos (6t) + β\\sin (6t))|= 3\\cos (6t); (-6α + 5β)\\sin (6t) + (5α + 6β)\\cos (6t)|= 3\\cos (6t)$\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-6α + 5β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (6t)) \\\\ 5α + 6β = 3\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (6t))$\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535122 false] [17592186228881 1145 "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $90\\cos(6t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor with resistance $30\\,\\Omega,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\dfrac1{150}\\,\\textrm{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\textrm d q}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 30\\,\\Omega.$ So,\n\n$$\nV_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nV_C = \\dfrac{q}{\\left(\\dfrac1{150}\\right)} = 150q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n90\\cos(6t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 150q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q = 3\\cos(6t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 5q_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nq_c(t) = Ae^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(6t) + \\beta\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(5\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac{15}{61}, \\beta = \\dfrac{18}{61}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nq_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-5t} + \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = \\dfrac{15}{61}\\cos(6t) + \\dfrac{18}{61}\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{15}{61}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{18}{61}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.38,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535122 true] [17592186229086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332251 13194139535122 true] [17592186229086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332258 13194139535122 true] [17592186229086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332265 13194139535122 true] [17592186229086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332272 13194139535122 true] [17592186229086 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332279 13194139535122 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332242 13194139535122 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332286 13194139535122 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332289 13194139535122 true] [17592186229087 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332292 13194139535122 true] [17592186229088 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332299 13194139535122 true] [17592186229088 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332306 13194139535122 true] [17592186229088 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332313 13194139535122 true] [17592186229088 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332320 13194139535122 true] [17592186229088 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332327 13194139535122 true] [17592186229650 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535122 true] [17592186229650 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe charge $q(t)$, in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dq}{dt} + 6q = \\frac{1}{2}$.\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139535122 false] [17592186229650 1100 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+6q=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139535122 true] [17592186229650 1145 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+6{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+6\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{1}{12}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{1}{12}$ coulombs." 13194139535122 true] [17592186332242 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332243 13194139535122 true] [17592186332242 1089 "q-336590" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332242 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535122 true] [17592186332242 1100 "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $24\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\nFind, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{80}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332242 1113 #uuid "70f691e9-1983-578a-ad38-54612631ed7b" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332242 1145 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=24\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=24I=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n15=24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{40}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{40}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{3}{40}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{80}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{80},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{3}{40} & =\\frac{1}{80} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25t/3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{3}{40}-\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-25(0.05)/3}≈0.034,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332244 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332245 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332246 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332247 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332248 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1041 "selection" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332247 13194139535122 true] [17592186332243 1110 #uuid "644135f7-c6a0-5f4f-b370-e523ea57caea" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332244 1034 "0.009" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332244 1111 #uuid "46f5d828-8aac-5c11-a700-6a224351dc02" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332245 1034 "0.028" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332245 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332245 1111 #uuid "f402933f-dbff-572b-900e-87080334bc6b" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332246 1034 "0.071" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332246 1111 #uuid "b5d5a913-a242-5a8b-8740-83f1589c540e" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332247 1034 "0.034" 13194139535122 true] [