ured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=60\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=60I=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.003}=\\frac{1000}{3}q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=60\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332281 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332282 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332283 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332284 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332285 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1041 "selection" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332283 13194139535122 true] [17592186332280 1110 #uuid "9f11723b-f889-53f3-a33f-03d1c8b5c510" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332281 1034 "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{9}{50}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332281 1111 #uuid "6e5a9d63-4a18-527e-b170-9a802d9a8066" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332282 1034 "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332282 1111 #uuid "c98350fd-f97b-555c-81bb-b8fb21864e5b" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332283 1034 "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{9}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332283 1111 #uuid "b1ebffca-1509-5de0-9a7a-5b0cf7414e01" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332284 1034 "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332284 1111 #uuid "9fc14736-920b-54ba-ba06-657fd9bc7155" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332285 1034 "\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{1000}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332285 1111 #uuid "0388246f-0794-59e0-9dcd-72ff5d336ad4" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332286 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332287 13194139535122 true] [17592186332286 1089 "q-337111" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332286 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535122 true] [17592186332286 1100 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." 13194139535122 true] [17592186332286 1113 #uuid "aa53f6c5-7b89-59b3-97f9-e5608374a440" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332286 1145 "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =\\frac{2}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{15}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{15}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{2}{15}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{2}{15}$ coulombs." 13194139535122 true] [17592186332287 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332288 13194139535122 true] [17592186332287 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535122 true] [17592186332287 1041 "field-1" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332287 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332288 13194139535122 true] [17592186332287 1110 #uuid "eacf1194-d909-547e-8612-617297057a96" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332288 1034 "\\frac{2}{15}" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535122 true] [17592186332288 1111 #uuid "385c993c-62c4-57b5-b931-292b2c3a6ab7" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332289 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332290 13194139535122 true] [17592186332289 1089 "q-337114" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535122 true] [17592186332289 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14049072/assets/b91e3582e017882aae67e8b6378074cf941be0c090d9f3f978076583b36c8837.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332289 1113 #uuid "0fa211b0-0cd5-5330-8aaa-5e30de3b98c3" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332289 1145 "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{4}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{25}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{25},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/4}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}+\\frac{6}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{125}(6-{e}^{-25t/4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{125}{e}^{-25t/4}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{6}{125}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." 13194139535122 true] [17592186332290 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332291 13194139535122 true] [17592186332290 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535122 true] [17592186332290 1041 "field-1" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332290 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332291 13194139535122 true] [17592186332290 1110 #uuid "bfec99a5-8afa-5aa9-b275-239fb1e48d40" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332291 1034 "0.048-0.008e^{-6.25t}" 13194139535122 true] [17592186332291 1103 #edb/ref 17592