}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{250}{27}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-250t/27},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{250}{27}\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{27}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{27}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-250t/27}+\\frac{27}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{90}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{90},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{90} \\\\ A{e}^{-250(0)/27}+\\frac{27}{500} & =\\frac{1}{90} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{193}{4500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{27}{500}-\\frac{193}{4500}{e}^{-250t/27}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{27}{500}-\\frac{193}{4500}{e}^{-250(0.05)/27}≈0.027,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535122 true] [17592186332293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332294 13194139535122 true] [17592186332293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332295 13194139535122 true] [17592186332293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332296 13194139535122 true] [17592186332293 100