s. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+8{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $6t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $6t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ (-6\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+(8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +8\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{25},\\beta =\\frac{9}{50}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general s